  :adr/body
  "## Position

**Nothing is on fire.** The deployment is healthy and self-healing. What is
undefined is **ownership** — no repo, no manifest and no team is responsible for
the source of a binary running on five machines. That decays quietly.

## The absence, confirmed — and one claim of mine softened

No `crates/` at the checkout, at the west pin, or on `origin/main`; confirmed by
`git ls-tree` and independently by the contents API. Not elsewhere in the fleet
either: a bounded search finds three `crates/` directories in 4,165 repos and
none holds this crate, and the name is unused in `west.yml`.

ADR-2608123600 said the source is *recoverable only from git history*. **That is
accurate; what I nearly wrote instead was not.** Locally the build commit
appeared to be reachable only from a stale side branch. Server-side comparison
is definitive: both the build pin and the deletion commit are **on `main`'s
history**, 643 and 584 commits back. So this is not one branch deletion from
gone, and GitHub retains it as long as `main` exists.

**The local appearance was an artifact of a defect worth fixing on its own:
`orgs/kotoba-lang/kotoba` is still a SHALLOW clone** — 24 graft boundaries, an
apparent history beginning 2026-07-14 with four false roots. CLAUDE.md records
that shallow was retired 2026-07-21 and everything unshallowed; **this repo was
missed.** It silently corrupts ancestry, `--contains` and path-limited `git log`
**in the one repo where this question keeps being asked.**

Second, related: its gitdir is a leftover *submodule* directory under the repo's
**old org path**, while the checkout lives under the new one. That explains an
internal contradiction in ADR-2607072000, which cites two different paths for
the same repo in two fields — the repo changed orgs and the local plumbing did
not.

## What is deployed, and the one thing that is missing

`bin/BUILD.edn` records the commit, the feature set and the target triple. It
was last modified 2026-06-27 and **cannot advance, because there are no further
Rust commits.** The binary is rsynced to the nodes, not built there, and runs as
a system LaunchDaemon with `RunAtLoad` and `KeepAlive`.

**There is no checksum binding artifact to commit.** A hash exists only as an
**8-character prefix in prose**, in a README and twice in an ADR — those three
mentions are its only occurrences, the full digest is recorded nowhere, and the
two records **disagree about whether it covers one binary or two.** So today the
position is: **we hope this is what it is.**

**The clearest existing cost is the watchdog.** A second daemon probes health
every 60 seconds and kills the process after two failures, and its own comment
names why: a 2026-07-02 incident where the HTTP surface wedged — accepting TCP,
never responding — while libp2p stayed alive, so `KeepAlive` could not heal it.
**That is a known defect being managed by a watchdog because nobody can rebuild
the binary to fix it.**

Also corroborated independently here: `KOTOBA_INTERNAL_SECRET` is **absent from
the deployment template**, which is ADR-2608123600's central claim arrived at
from the other direction.

## Rebuildability: the inputs are unusually good, bit-reproduction is impossible

At the build commit the tree is complete — 37 workspace members, 41 source files
in this crate — and the lockfile is **v4 with 1,056 packages, 1,020 from
crates.io and `source = \"git+\"` appearing exactly ZERO times.** No git
dependencies is the single best fact available: crates.io is immutable and
serves yanked versions, so the closure is very likely still fetchable. That was
**inferred, not measured** — no fetch was run — and it is cheap to settle.

Against that: **no `rust-toolchain.toml`**, so the compiler version is unpinned;
and `.cargo/config.toml` sets `target-cpu=native`, so **a rebuild on different
silicon is never byte-identical.** A recorded checksum can therefore
**authenticate** the deployed artifact but can never be **re-derived** by
rebuilding. It also confirms the five copies came from one build fanned out by
rsync.

## Restoring the source does not violate the no-Rust ADR

Read closely, ADR-2607072000 makes three decisions: cljc is the default for
**new** implementation; **explicitly no wholesale retroactive rewrite**; and
`kotoba-server` is named as the top-priority gap and **deliberately not
started**, because it runs in production and needs a dedicated ADR with staged
cutover and rollback first.

Storing the already-written source of an already-running binary is neither new
Rust implementation nor a rewrite. **The ADR's own logic points the other way:**
it demands rollback, and **rollback requires the artifact you roll back to be
identifiable.** Preserving the source serves that policy rather than defying it.

Replacement has already been adjudicated twice on real hardware and should not
be reopened as a way of solving ownership. ADR-2607082400 tested a cljc
replacement and rejected drop-in on two verified walls: the only JVM WASM engine
in use rejects the fleet's guests at the **binary version header** (they are
Component Model, not core WASM) and no JVM component-model runtime was found;
and the cljc libp2p is 215 lines of routing math whose own docstring says it
**does not open sockets, dial peers, or perform any handshake.** ADR-2607072100's
strangler-fig roadmap is the sanctioned path but **names two repos that do not
appear in `west.yml`.** And the one node where the Rust daemon was replaced runs
a fixed single **unauthenticated** route, with that ADR stating plainly that
re-provisioning will **silently restore the Rust plist.**

Measured: replacement is a staked claim, not a substitute.

## Recommendation

**(a) and (b) together.** Cheap, non-exclusive, and neither commits anyone to a
direction on Rust.

**(a) Give the source an address.** By `manifest/repository-rules.edn`'s
`:plane-order`: origin does not apply (our own code), role does not apply (no
prefix covers a daemon), **family does** — so `kotoba-lang/kotoba-server`, free
in `west.yml`. The org/name duplication is fine: the elision rule is explicitly
subordinate to existing family form and this family has 32 members. The `-clj`
prohibition is not engaged — do not append a language suffix. A registered
`archived` group already exists, so it need not be fetched by default. **This
does not restart Rust development; it gives the running binary an address.**

**(b) Record the artifact durably.** Compute the full sha256 of each binary on a
node, record them against the build commit in `BUILD.edn` (git-tracked, already
the right place), and put the artifact in B2 + git-annex per the
`large-binary-datalad` skill with custody verification. **State in the record
that the build is not bit-reproducible**, so the hash reads as an authenticity
anchor and not a reproducibility claim.

**(d) Retiring** is weaker than it looks but worth pricing — one measurement
recorded zero peers, a README warns its topology column is history rather than
current peering, and half the fleet was never provisioned. **Sequencing matters:
do (a) and (b) first regardless. Retiring a binary you cannot identify is how
you lose the ability to bring it back.**

## An unauthenticated route that rewrites the fleet's routing table

Found while assessing whether the internal-trust secret could be enabled, and it
is more serious than the two read routes ADR-2608123600 named.

Those two — `/mesh/http/kenchi/valuation` and `/mesh/http/minidrama/profile` —
take no `HeaderMap` parameter at all, so authentication is not merely absent but
**impossible**. Both are documented as deliberately read-only on-mesh surfaces
serving governor-cleared records, with the governed pipeline kept off-mesh. **So
the missing auth on those two looks intended.**

**`mesh_deploy`, which INSTALLS those routes, has the same signature and is also
unauthenticated.** It feeds a handler that installs routes with no signature
check, no publisher identity and no authorization, and **replaces the entire
routing table** rather than merging. It both applies locally and gossips to
every peer. So an unauthenticated POST to any node can replace that node's mesh
routing and propagate it fleet-wide; the remaining brake is only that the
component artifact must resolve through the local block store or bitswap.

Compounding it: the invoke path's own doc comment states that a component is *a
real writer to the kotoba graph, not a no-op sandbox* — it persists guest
assertions into whatever graph the guest names, with no owner check, quota or
fee.

**The fleet build enables this**: the recorded feature set includes `p2p`, and
both routes are gated on that feature.

## Enabling the internal-trust secret is a change of security model, not a config tweak

Assessed and **deliberately not done.** Four reasons, in order of weight:

1. **There is no secret to read, and creating one is not mine.** The value
   exists only on the cloud path. No fleet secret has ever existed. Minting one
   is generating a credential; reusing the gateway's would silently extend one
   credential's blast radius from a single edge to ten LAN daemons. So no
   credential store was queried at all — **there was no identified item to
   fetch**, which is the condition under which the safety floor says to ask
   rather than guess.
2. **The blast radius is larger than the three bridges named, and not fully
   knowable from the repo.** Seven bridges, not three, spread across four orgs
   rather than one — **none sends the header**. Plus murakumo itself: its own
   identity module mints exactly this unsigned token and attaches it to state
   writes, so enabling the gate would break the fleet's own recording, **and
   because that write is a background path it would fail quietly.** Three
   further callers have no allowlist at all.
3. **It is a fail-closed cutover with no warn-only mode**, and whether a given
   actor's loopback IS a fleet node cannot be determined without the running
   fleet.
4. **The design intent does not match the topology.** The security package
   defines this as the *edge-to-origin* boundary, with a go-live checklist
   reading `secret set; raw origin IP not routable`. The murakumo fleet has no
   edge. On the fleet the variable stops meaning *you came through the edge* and
   starts meaning *you hold a bearer secret*.

That fourth point is also why it may be **the highest-value fix available here**
— it converts a published, unverifiable, never-expiring DID into a real
credential. But it is a security-model change and belongs to the owner.

**The safe ordering, for whenever that happens: clients first.** Sending the
header to a server with the secret unset is a complete no-op, so all seven
bridges and murakumo's write path can be updated and shipped harmlessly; only
once every caller demonstrably sends it does enabling the server side become low
risk. **The other order breaks everything at once.**

## What could not be determined

- **Whether the deployed binary matches the build commit.** The artifact is not
  on this machine and no node was contacted. (b) is what closes this.
- **The full sha256** — only an 8-character prefix exists, and the two records
  disagree on what it covers.
- **Whether the 1,020 crates.io dependencies still resolve** — highly likely,
  but inferred rather than measured.
- **Live fleet state.** Every deployment number is read from documents measured
  2026-07-25 by someone else and was not re-verified; no network probing."}]
